.Dd September 5, 2026
.Dt KFIRE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kfire
.Nd clone Firecracker microVMs from an appliance's ZFS zvols
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm golden
.Ar vm
.Op Fl -force
.Nm
.Cm goldens
.Nm
.Cm golden-destroy
.Ar golden
.Nm
.Cm clone
.Ar golden
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl -name Ar prefix
.Op Fl -cpu Ar n
.Op Fl -ram Ar mb
.Op Fl -bridge Ar br
.Op Fl -wait
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm stop
.Ar name | Fl -all
.Nm
.Cm start
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm destroy
.Ar name | Fl -all
.Nm
.Cm ssh
.Ar name
.Op Ar command
.Nm
.Cm console
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm status
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
turns a kldload appliance VM into a golden and clones copies of it as
Firecracker microVMs.
The golden is built once, the slow way: a full libvirt first boot with
cloud-init, the recipe, verification and enrollment, exactly as
.Xr vmxplore 1
does it.
Each copy is then a ZFS clone of the golden's zvols plus a Firecracker
process, which is a few hundred milliseconds of work and a few hundred
megabytes of host memory per instance.
Nothing inside the guest changes: it is the same disk, the same kernel and
the same services, on a different virtual machine monitor.
.Pp
Every instance is registered in the estate database the way the appliances
are, runs under Firecracker's jailer as the unprivileged
.Sy kfire
user in a chroot of its own, as a systemd unit named
.Ql kfire- Ns Ar name ,
and is removed by
.Cm destroy
together with its zvols, tap device, seed and database row, so the estate
never carries a microVM that nothing can see or take away.
.Ss Verbs
.Bl -tag -width "golden-destroy"
.It Cm golden Ar vm Op Fl -force
Snapshot the shut-off VM's root zvol, and its data zvol when it has one, as
.Ql @kfire ,
and copy the guest's kernel and initramfs out of its root filesystem into
.Pa /var/lib/kfire/goldens/ Ns Ar vm .
The VM must be shut off; a golden of a running VM is crash-consistent and
.Fl -force
says that is acceptable.
Firecracker releases before 1.17 boot only an uncompressed ELF kernel, so
the bzImage is unpacked with the kernel tree's own
.Pa scripts/extract-vmlinux
when a kernel-devel or linux-headers tree is present, and by the same
search done inline otherwise.
.It Cm goldens
List goldens with their size, root device and clone count.
.It Cm golden-destroy Ar golden
Drop a golden.
ZFS refuses to destroy a snapshot with clones, and that refusal is the guard:
.Cm destroy
the instances first.
.It Cm clone Ar golden Op Ar options
Clone one or more instances.
Each gets a clone of the root zvol and of the data zvol, a NoCloud seed
carrying its instance-id and hostname, a tap on the bridge owned by the
.Sy kfire
user, a locally administered MAC derived from its name, and a Firecracker
configuration.
Then the jailer builds a chroot under
.Pa /var/lib/kfire/jail
with the kernel, initramfs and seed hard-linked in, the zvols as device
nodes the jail user owns, and a copy of the Firecracker binary, and starts
the microVM there as a transient systemd unit, seccomp on, API socket inside
the jail.
Without a jailer on PATH, or with
.Ev KFIRE_NO_JAIL
set, Firecracker runs as root under systemd with its socket in
.Pa /run/kfire .
.Bl -tag -width "--bridge br"
.It Fl n , Fl -count Ar count
How many.
Names are numbered from 1, skipping any that exist.
.It Fl -name Ar prefix
Instance name prefix; the default is the golden's name without its
.Ql app-
prefix, so
.Ql app-web-stack
clones
.Ql web-stack-1 .
.It Fl -cpu Ar n , Fl -ram Ar mb
Per instance.
The default is the golden VM's own size.
Firecracker commits guest memory lazily, so the RAM figure is a ceiling
rather than a reservation.
.It Fl -bridge Ar br
The bridge the tap joins; the default
.Ql virbr0
is libvirt's network, whose DHCP gives the instance an address that
.Cm list
can read.
.It Fl -wait
Stay until every cloned instance answers on the golden's port, and print
the milliseconds each one took from the start of the run.
Exit 1 if any does not within three minutes.
.El
.It Cm list Op Fl -json
Every instance with its state, address, golden, size and tap.
.Fl -json
is the form
.Xr vmxplore 1
reads.
.It Cm stop Ar name | Fl -all
Shut the guest down: Ctrl-Alt-Del over the API socket, which the guest
turns into a reboot that Firecracker exits on; SIGTERM only after sixty
seconds of that being ignored.
A stopped instance keeps its zvols and identity.
.It Cm start Ar name
Start a stopped instance again with the same zvols, seed and MAC.
.It Cm destroy Ar name | Fl -all
Stop, then remove the zvols, the tap, the seed, the unit and the estate
database row.
There is no undo.
.It Cm ssh Ar name Op Ar command
Root over the host's ops key, the one every kldload appliance seeds at
build time.
.It Cm console Ar name
Follow the serial console log.
.It Cm status
Firecracker version, bridge state, counts.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
See the verbs above; every option belongs to
.Cm clone .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "KFIRE_FIRECRACKER" -compact
.It Ev KFIRE_POOL
The dataset under which appliance zvols live; default
.Ql rpool/vms .
.It Ev KFIRE_BRIDGE
Default bridge for
.Cm clone ;
.Ql virbr0 .
.It Ev KFIRE_FIRECRACKER
The firecracker binary; default
.Ql firecracker
on PATH.
.It Ev KFIRE_NO_JAIL
Set to
.Ql 1 ,
run Firecracker as root under systemd rather than jailed — for a host with
no jailer, or to take the jail out of a diagnosis.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/lib/kfire/instances/<name>/" -compact
.It Pa /var/lib/kfire/goldens/<golden>/
.Pa vmlinux ,
.Pa initrd.img
and
.Pa golden.json
(zvols, snapshot, root device, size, port).
.It Pa /var/lib/kfire/instances/<name>/
.Pa config.json ,
.Pa seed.iso ,
.Pa instance.json
and
.Pa console.log .
.It Pa /var/lib/kfire/jail/firecracker/<name>/root/
The instance's chroot: kernel, initramfs, seed, zvol nodes, the binary,
and
.Pa run/firecracker.socket ,
its API socket.
.It Pa /run/kfire/<name>.sock
The API socket when not jailed.
.It Pa rpool/vms/<name> , Pa rpool/vms/<name>-data
The instance's zvol clones, under the pool
.Ev KFIRE_POOL
names.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
Failure: a missing binary, zvol, golden or instance; a clone whose unit died
at once; an instance that did not answer under
.Fl -wait .
.It 2
Usage: an unknown verb or option, a missing argument.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Take a golden from the Web Stack appliance, which must be shut off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire golden app-web-stack
golden app-web-stack: snapshotting rpool/vms/app-web-stack@kfire and rpool/vms/app-web-stack-data@kfire
  kernel: vmlinuz-7.1.13-200.fc44.x86_64 \(-> ELF vmlinux (73 MB)
golden app-web-stack ready: root=/dev/vda3 rootflags=subvol=root 2 vCPU / 2048 MB + data pool
.Ed
.Pp
Clone ten and wait for them, as measured on a 24-core, 32 GB desktop:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire clone app-web-stack -n 10 --wait
web-stack-1  cloned in 195 ms  (2 vCPU / 2048 MB, aa:fc:ac:a1:b7:20 on virbr0)
\&...
10 cloned in 5014 ms, 0 failed
web-stack-1          192.168.122.33   http 200  answering at  13105 ms
\&...
web-stack-7          192.168.122.27   http 200  answering at  33398 ms
all 10 answering · host: 8796 MB available
.Ed
.Pp
Three at a larger size, on the LAN bridge, named for a customer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire clone app-web-stack -n 3 --name acme --cpu 4 --ram 4096 --bridge br0
.Ed
.Pp
See what is running and where:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire list
web-stack-1          running   192.168.122.33   app-web-stack    2 vCPU /  2048 MB  fc-web-stack-1
web-stack-2          shut off  -                app-web-stack    2 vCPU /  2048 MB  fc-web-stack-2
.Ed
.Pp
Check the services inside one, stop it cleanly, start it again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire ssh web-stack-1 'systemctl is-active nginx postgresql valkey; zpool list -H -o name,health'
active
active
active
tank    ONLINE
# kfire stop web-stack-1
web-stack-1 shut off
# kfire start web-stack-1
web-stack-1 started
.Ed
.Pp
Watch a first boot, then remove everything:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire console web-stack-3
# kfire destroy --all
web-stack-1 destroyed
web-stack-2 destroyed
web-stack-3 destroyed
.Ed
.Pp
The machine-readable form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kfire list --json | jq -r '.[] | "\\(.name) \\(.state) \\(.ip)"'
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It "firecracker not on PATH"
A kldload KVM host installs it at firstboot from the baked tarball; a host
built before that, or with the tarball missing and no network, has to
install the release binary by hand.
.It "is <state> \(em a golden is taken from a shut-off VM"
Shut the VM down first, or pass
.Fl -force
for a crash-consistent golden.
.It "could not extract an ELF vmlinux"
No kernel-devel or linux-headers tree for
.Pa scripts/extract-vmlinux
and none of the inline decompressors matched.
Install one, or Firecracker 1.17 or later, which boots the bzImage itself.
.It "<name> FAILED to start"
Firecracker rejected the configuration and the unit ended within
milliseconds; the first error line from its console follows.
The instance stays on disk for
.Cm console
and
.Cm destroy .
.It "<n> of <m> not answering on :<port>"
Under
.Fl -wait ,
a guest that has not answered within three minutes.
.Cm console
shows its first boot.
.It "<zvol> still exists (busy)"
The block device was still held a few seconds after the process ended;
destroy it by hand.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr systemd-run 1 ,
.Xr vmxplore 1 ,
.Xr kexport 8 ,
.Xr kspawn 8 ,
.Xr kvm-mesh 8 ,
.Xr zfs 8
.Sh HISTORY
Written on 2026-09-05 after the Web Stack tile, four minutes to build under
libvirt, cloned in 250 milliseconds and served HTTP seven seconds later
under Firecracker.
The first attempt landed in dracut's emergency shell because Firecracker
appends its own
.Ql root=/dev/vda
after the boot arguments when a drive is marked the root device, and the
kernel takes the last one.
.Sh CAVEATS
Firecracker offers no PCI passthrough, no GPU, no display and no USB.
Tiles that need a radio, a tuner, a desktop or sound stay on libvirt.
.Pp
The jail is a chroot, an unprivileged uid and seccomp; it is not a network
namespace, so the tap still lives in the host's namespace on the bridge the
instance was given.
.Pp
The libvirt network's DHCP hands out addresses and
.Xr kldload-networks 8
does not touch these rows, so an instance's address in the estate database
is the one
.Cm clone Fl -wait
or
.Cm start
last recorded.
.Pp
Ten first boots at once contend for the host's cores: three instances
answered in thirteen seconds and ten in thirty-three on the same machine.
